# **The place the server picks when it is told none**, which is the only thing
# this session is about: every other one is handed a PASLS_SCRATCH by the
# harness, so none of them can see it. The `.tmpdir` marker leaves the variable
# unset and gives the server an empty TMPDIR of its own. What is checked is
# seen from *inside the compiler* while the server runs -- a directory of the
# server's own, `pasls-XXXXXX`, made for it by the system with the document
# inside -- and then that TMPDIR is empty again after `exit` (ADR-0363).
#
# It was one file, `pasls-<the server's pid>.pas`, at the top of TMPDIR
# (ADR-0242): a name without the pid had let two servers on one machine write
# each other's documents, and the pid closed that. What it did not close was
# everybody else -- a name anyone sharing the directory can compose is a name
# they can plant a link at first, and the compiler then truncates whatever the
# link names. The mutation is to compose the name at the top again: this
# session is the one thing that fails.
#
# The document is deliberately correct, because the scratch file is written
# whether or not the compilation says anything -- and a session that also
# pinned a diagnostic would be pinning two things at once.
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"processId":null,"rootUri":null,"capabilities":{}}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"textDocument/didOpen","params":{"textDocument":{"uri":"file:///scratch/quiet.pas","languageId":"pascal","version":1,"text":"program quiet(output);\nbegin\n  writeln('quiet')\nend.\n"}}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"shutdown"}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"exit"}
